{"@context":["https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/ns/content/v1.jsonld",{"@base":"https://litlfred.github.io/folio/","folio-assistant-core":"https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant-core/v0/ns/"}],"@id":"papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/rem-on-p-modular-hecke-significance","@type":["folio-assistant-core:Remark","doco:Section"],"label":"rem:on-p-modular-hecke-significance","kind":"remark","title":"Significance of the smallest modular-Hecke nimber case","uses":["papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/conj-on-p-modular-hecke"],"interprets":"papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/conj-on-p-modular-hecke","tags":["archimedean-bridge","surreal-numbers","nimbers","modular-hecke","significance"],"text":"on-p-modular-hecke-significance.md","provenance":"authored","html":"<a id=\"rem:on-p-modular-hecke-significance\"></a><p><strong>Archimedean specialisation.</strong> Even verifying the smallest case (<code class=\"language-math math-inline\">p = 2, n = 3</code>) of the <a href=\"#conj:on-p-modular-hecke\">modular Hecke <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\leftrightarrow</code> nimber correspondence</a> would be the first explicit connection between Hecke combinatorics and nim-arithmetic, and the entry point to a larger program.</p>\n","source":"folio/quantum-observable-universe/appendix-surreals/on-p-modular-hecke-significance.md","heading":"Remark","number":"28.11.6","page":"b/rem-on-p-modular-hecke-significance","qa":{"counts":{"pass":45,"fail":0,"warn":0,"na":17,"unknown":0,"stale":61}}}
